speed, timing, choke, throttle linkage, and distributor cap. The battery is typically checked during a tune-up as well since it is a crucial component with a limited life span. During a thorough inspection, your mechanic is also likel ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/the-importance-of-regularly-scheduled-tune-ups" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<center><p><img alt="" height="299" src="/Files/EmailCampaigns/auto-tune-up.jpg" style="width: 450px; height: 299px;" width="450" /></p></center><p>[Updated 9/26/2020] To keep your car running smoothly, efficiently, and safely, it is extremely important to follow <b>scheduled maintenance</b> and get regular tune-ups. When your vehicle is properly tuned, you are likely to experience <b>better performance</b>, improved <b>emissions,</b> and increased <b>mileage</b>.</p><p>A <b>tune-up</b> is basically a check-up of your vehicle by a skilled mechanic. During a tune-up, all your vehicle&rsquo;s important systems are checked and fixed as needed. Things that are commonly checked include spark plugs and wires, idle speed, timing, choke, throttle linkage, and distributor cap. The battery is typically checked during a tune-up as well since it is a crucial component with a limited life span.</p><p>During a thorough inspection, your mechanic is also likely to check your brakes, belts, exhaust system, transmission, and tires. Simply checking the fluids and the air pressure in a vehicle&rsquo;s tires often leads to noticeable improvements in fuel economy.</p><p>Most new vehicles require very little when it comes to tune-ups as long as <b>routine maintenance</b> and <b>oil changes</b> are being performed as recommended.</p><p>This entry was posted in Tune Up Blog. Bookmark the permalink.</p> ]]></content><media:thumbnail url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/auto-tune-up.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/auto-tune-up.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Auto tune up.jpg</media:title></media:content><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/auto-tune-up.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Auto tune up.jpg</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[Buying a Used Car: What You Need to Know]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/buying-a-used-car--what-you-need-to-know</link><pubDate>10/14/2014 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p>[Updated 9/26/2020] When you&rsquo;re in the market for a new set of wheels, <b>buying a used car</b> rather than a new one makes a lot of financial sense. In addition to the upfront savings, buying used also means you avoid the depreciation that occurs the instant a new car leaves the dealer&rsquo;s lot. All the money-saving benefits of buying used go right out the window, though, if the car you get doesn&rsquo;t turn out to be a good deal or has a lot of problems. Keep these tips in mind to be sure you choose a good new-to-you car. <strong>Common Problems to Watch Out For</strong> While it can be difficult to detect every possible problem with a vehicle, there are a few major signs of potentially costly problems. Many of these things can be detected during a test drive. Take the car on ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/buying-a-used-car--what-you-need-to-know" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<p>[Updated 9/26/2020] When you&rsquo;re in the market for a new set of wheels, <b>buying a used car</b> rather than a new one makes a lot of financial sense. In addition to the upfront savings, buying used also means you avoid the depreciation that occurs the instant a new car leaves the dealer&rsquo;s lot. All the money-saving benefits of buying used go right out the window, though, if the car you get doesn&rsquo;t turn out to be a good deal or has a lot of problems. Keep these tips in mind to be sure you choose a good new-to-you car.</p><center><p><img alt="Buying used car" src="/Files/Images/Blog/buying-a-used-car.jpg" /></p></center><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Common Problems to Watch Out For</strong></span></h3><p>While it can be difficult to detect every possible problem with a vehicle, there are a few major signs of potentially costly problems. Many of these things can be detected during a test drive. Take the car on a test drive on different types of roads to see how it handles. Pay close attention to how well it corners and brakes. Leave the radio turned off and listen for any squealing or knocking sounds.</p><p><b>Leaking fluids</b> is a common sign of potential problems, and they can be detected during a test drive. While you&rsquo;re driving, stop the vehicle on a clean area of pavement or cement, such as in a parking lot. Leave the vehicle idle for at least 30 seconds, then move ahead. Check the area where you were just parked for any fluids. Black fluid indicates an <b>oil leak</b> while <b>pink fluid</b> is a sign of a transmission leak, and <b>green fluid</b> is antifreeze.</p><p>Look under the hood, and thoroughly inspect the vehicle inside and out. The engine compartment shouldn&rsquo;t look like it was just pressure washed, but the components also should not be filthy and rusty. Check underneath the car for rust damage. Pay attention to the cleanliness of the passenger compartment. The way people care for their car&rsquo;s interior is often a reflection of how well they maintain the vehicle&rsquo;s other components as well.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Do a Bit of Homework</strong></span></h3><p>Before going to check out used cars, do a little bit of research. Come up with a rough idea of the type of vehicle you are looking for, and check online to find out what the average selling price should be. Also, do a quick search to learn more about any problems associated with certain makes and models in which you are interested.</p><p>When looking at a particular vehicle, keep in mind what your research told you. The asking price should be within the average range of price you found on similar vehicles. If it is significantly higher, the seller may be trying to rip you off. Beware of considerably underpriced vehicles, too, as the low price tag may be a reflection of structural or mechanical problems the seller hasn&rsquo;t disclosed.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Get a Professional Opinion</strong></span></h3><p>Whenever possible, taking a used car you are interested in to a <b>mechanic</b> prior to making a purchase is a good idea. Even if you are buying from a dealership, taking the car to your own mechanic is advised. A skilled mechanic can look over the vehicle to find any existing and potential problems. He can also advise you on problems commonly associated with the vehicle you are thinking about purchasing.</p><p>If there are current problems that need to be addressed, you can get an estimate of how much it would cost to get the vehicle fully road-ready. With this information in mind, you can make a more informed decision on how much you are willing to spend on the vehicle or if you want it at all. Knowing a vehicle needs a considerable amount of work can work in your favor when it comes time to negotiate. If you have a written estimate for necessary repairs, the seller may be willing to come down on the price.</p><p>If you are thinking about <b>buying a used car</b>, bring it to one of the professional mechanics right here at Tune Tech Mechanic. We will be glad to help you make an informed decision when it comes to making a purchase, and we hope to be the garage you turn to for the life of your vehicle. Call us today to schedule your appointment.</p><p>&nbsp;</p> ]]></content><media:thumbnail url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/buying-a-used-car.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/buying-a-used-car.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Buying a used car.jpg</media:title></media:content><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/buying-a-used-car.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Buying used car</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[Are You Hearing Scary Car Problem Sounds?]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/are-you-hearing-scary-car-problem-sounds-</link><pubDate>10/17/2014 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p> A strange car noise is useful information. Grinding, squealing, clunking, humming, knocking, or thumping can point toward brakes, tires, suspension, belts, bearings, exhaust, or engine problems. Updated June 2026 Reading time: 8 min Are You Hearing Scary Car Problem Sounds? This guide updates our original scary-sounds post into a practical noise-triage checklist for Aurora drivers. Use it to decide what to describe to the shop, which sounds deserve a fast inspection, and when it is smarter to stop driving and call. <strong>The short answer:</strong> Do not ignore new noises. Grinding while braking, knocking from the engine, a loud tire thump, a wheel growl that changes while turning, or any noise paired with smoke, warning lights, overheating, steering changes, or brake ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/are-you-hearing-scary-car-problem-sounds-" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<div class="blog-content"><p class="blog-index-lede">A strange car noise is useful information. Grinding, squealing, clunking, humming, knocking, or thumping can point toward brakes, tires, suspension, belts, bearings, exhaust, or engine problems.</p><span class="update-badge">Updated June 2026</span><div class="post-meta"><span>Reading time: 8 min</span></div><h1>Are You Hearing Scary Car Problem Sounds?</h1><p>This guide updates our original scary-sounds post into a practical noise-triage checklist for Aurora drivers. Use it to decide what to describe to the shop, which sounds deserve a fast inspection, and when it is smarter to stop driving and call.</p><div class="key-takeaway"><p><strong>The short answer:</strong> Do not ignore new noises. Grinding while braking, knocking from the engine, a loud tire thump, a wheel growl that changes while turning, or any noise paired with smoke, warning lights, overheating, steering changes, or brake changes should be inspected quickly.</p><p>A noise does not diagnose the car by itself, but it tells a mechanic where to start testing.</p></div><div class="toc"><h3>In This Guide</h3><ul><li><a href="#stop-driving">When to Stop Driving</a></li><li><a href="#sounds">Common Car Noises and What They Can Mean</a></li><li><a href="#describe">How to Describe the Noise</a></li><li><a href="#diagnosis">What a Noise Diagnosis Looks Like</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Car Noise Questions</a></li></ul></div><div class="trust-indicators"><div class="trust-item">Serving Aurora since 1978</div><div class="trust-item">ASE-certified mechanics</div><div class="trust-item">NAPA AutoCare Center</div><div class="trust-item">24/24 nationwide warranty</div></div><h2 id="stop-driving">When a Car Noise Means You Should Stop Driving</h2><p>Some noises are annoying. Others are safety warnings. If the vehicle feels different, smells different, or shows a warning light at the same time, treat the noise seriously.</p><div class="warning-box"><p><strong>Stop driving and call for help if you notice:</strong></p><ul><li>Grinding brakes, a sinking brake pedal, or a brake warning light</li><li>Engine knocking, oil pressure warning, or heavy tapping from the engine</li><li>A loud tire thump, wobble, pull, or tire pressure warning with visible damage</li><li>Smoke, steam, fuel smell, coolant smell, or overheating</li><li>A flashing check engine light, severe misfire, or loss of power</li><li>Clunking or popping that changes steering, braking, or control</li></ul></div><p>If you are unsure whether the car is safe to drive, call before driving it farther. A tow is frustrating. A brake, tire, steering, or engine failure on Colfax, I-225, or I-70 is worse.</p><h2 id="sounds">Common Car Noises and What They Can Mean</h2><p>One noise can have more than one cause, so use this table as a starting point, not a final diagnosis. The location, speed, temperature, and driving condition matter.</p><table class="cost-table"><thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Common areas to check</th><th>What to do next</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Grinding or scraping while braking</strong></td><td>Brake pads, rotors, calipers, backing plates, hardware</td><td>Schedule <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/brakes">brake inspection</a> quickly. Stop driving if braking feels weak, low, or uneven.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Squealing when braking</strong></td><td>Brake pad wear indicators, glazed pads, dust, hardware</td><td>Do not wait for the sound to become grinding. Pads caught early can prevent rotor damage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Squeal or chirp under the hood</strong></td><td>Serpentine belt, tensioner, pulleys, accessories</td><td>Have the belt drive checked, especially if the sound changes with engine speed or weather.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clunk or pop over bumps</strong></td><td>Suspension joints, struts, shocks, sway bar links, steering parts</td><td>Use caution. A <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/suspension-systems">suspension inspection</a> can find loose or worn parts before handling gets worse.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Humming or growling that changes with speed</strong></td><td>Wheel bearings, tires, wheel balance, drivetrain parts</td><td>Note whether turning left or right changes the sound. That clue can help isolate a wheel bearing or tire issue.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thumping from a wheel or tire</strong></td><td>Low tire, damaged tire, separated tread, loose wheel, brake or suspension issue</td><td>Pull over safely if the vehicle shakes or pulls. Inspect tires before continuing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Knocking, tapping, or rattling from the engine</strong></td><td>Oil level, internal engine wear, timing components, misfire, loose shields</td><td>Check warning lights and oil level if safe. Stop driving if the oil light is on or the knock is heavy.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hissing, sizzling, or steam sounds</strong></td><td>Coolant leak, overheating, vacuum leak, exhaust leak</td><td>Do not open a hot cooling system. Stop if temperature rises or steam appears.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Whining during turns or acceleration</strong></td><td>Power steering system, belt drive, transmission, differential, wheel bearings</td><td>Pay attention to when it happens. Turning, shifting, and throttle changes point to different systems.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="colorado-tip"><strong>Tire Safety Note</strong><p>The <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/tires" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Highway Traffic Safety Administration</a> says poor tire maintenance, including low pressure and worn tread, can lead to flats, blowouts, or tread separation. If a noise feels like it is coming from a tire or wheel, inspect it before driving far.</p></div><h2 id="describe">How to Describe a Car Noise to the Shop</h2><p>You do not need to know the name of the failed part. A good description of the symptom is often more useful than a guess.</p><p>Before you call, think through these details:</p><ul><li><strong>When does it happen?</strong> Startup, idle, acceleration, braking, turning, bumps, highway speed, reverse, cold mornings, or after the car warms up?</li><li><strong>Where does it seem to come from?</strong> Front, rear, left, right, under the hood, under the car, inside the dash, or one wheel?</li><li><strong>Does speed change it?</strong> A sound that gets faster with wheel speed points differently than one that follows engine rpm.</li><li><strong>Does braking or turning change it?</strong> Brakes, wheel bearings, suspension, and steering problems often reveal themselves this way.</li><li><strong>What else changed?</strong> Warning lights, vibration, smoke, smell, low fluid, weak acceleration, pulling, or poor fuel economy?</li></ul><p>A short phone video can help if it captures the sound clearly and safely. Do not record while driving. If a passenger can record, keep it short and note speed, road condition, and whether you were braking or turning.</p><h2 id="diagnosis">What a Noise Diagnosis Looks Like</h2><p>Good diagnosis starts by confirming the symptom. Depending on the sound, a technician may road test the vehicle, scan for codes, inspect it on a lift, check tire condition, look for loose shields, test wheel bearings, inspect brakes, or look for leaks and worn suspension parts.</p><p>That matters because replacing the part that sounds likely is not the same as fixing the car. A humming noise could be a tire, bearing, or drivetrain issue. A squeal could be belt-related or brake-related. A rattle could be harmless heat-shield noise or a sign that something is loose where it should not be.</p><p>If the sound comes with rough running, hesitation, a flashing check engine light, or poor fuel economy, start with <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/auto-diagnostic">auto diagnostics</a>. If it comes with braking symptoms, start with brakes. If it happens over bumps or while turning, start with suspension and steering.</p><div class="faq-section" id="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"><h2>Car Noise Questions, Answered</h2><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Is it safe to drive with a grinding noise?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">A grinding noise should be inspected quickly, especially if it happens while braking, turning, or accelerating. Stop driving if braking feels weak, the vehicle pulls, warning lights appear, or the sound is loud metal-on-metal.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">What does squealing when I brake usually mean?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Squealing during braking often points to brake pad wear indicators, pad material, dust, or hardware. It is better to inspect it before it turns into grinding because grinding can mean metal contact and rotor damage.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Why does my car hum or growl at highway speed?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">A hum or growl that changes with speed can come from tires, wheel bearings, wheel balance, or drivetrain parts. Note whether turning left or right changes the sound, then schedule an inspection.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">What does clunking over bumps mean?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Clunking over bumps can come from worn suspension or steering parts, struts, shocks, sway bar links, control arms, or loose hardware. Because those systems affect control, have repeat clunks checked.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Can I diagnose a car noise from a code reader?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">A code reader can help if the noise comes with a check engine light, but many noises do not set codes. Brakes, tires, suspension, belts, bearings, exhaust, and loose hardware usually need inspection and testing.</p></div></div></div><div class="review-section"><h2>What Aurora Drivers Say About Noise Diagnostics</h2><p class="review-subtitle">Real reviews from Tune Tech customers</p><div class="review-grid"><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"I took in my car for a noise and safety issue... The diagnosis was clear and concise (with videos provided!). The shop helped me prioritize my fixes... the biggest issues were solved, and I'm now driving a safer and quiter vehicle."</div><div class="review-author">J. L. R.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"Selected Tune Tech To Service an Intermittent squeal/vibration in the front end of my suv... he let me know that they couldn't be sure till they looked at my vehicle. Lo and behold... it was only a bad wheel bearing."</div><div class="review-author">S. M.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"I asked Ken to check out a noise I was hearing under the car. He spent at least 45 minutes checking the problem and taking the car for a test run. Found out it was a problem with a bearing..."</div><div class="review-author">C. C.</div></a></div><p class="review-link"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/tmoHot2rVJYyiGTk7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View more reviews on Google &rarr;</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews">See reviews on our site &rarr;</a></p></div><div class="cta-section"><h2>Hear a Noise You Do Not Want to Ignore?</h2><p>ASE-certified mechanics. NAPA AutoCare Center. Serving Aurora since 1978.</p><div class="cta-buttons"><a class="cta-primary" href="tel:3033279176">Call (303) 327-9176</a></div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json"> { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article", "headline": "Are You Hearing Scary Car Problem Sounds?", "description": "Hear grinding, squealing, knocking, humming, or thumping? Learn what common car noises can mean and when Aurora drivers should stop driving and call.", "image": [ "https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/car-noise-diagnostics-square.jpg" ], "datePublished": "2014-10-17T08:00:00-06:00", "dateModified": "2026-09-02T08:00:00-06:00", "wordCount": 1430, "articleSection": "Car Problems & Diagnostics", "keywords": "car noises, scary car sounds, grinding noise when braking, squealing brakes, clunking noise over bumps, wheel bearing hum, engine knocking, suspension noise, Aurora auto diagnostic", "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Tune Tech Automotive" }, "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Tune Tech Automotive", "url": "https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/logo.png" } }, "mainEntityOfPage": "https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/are-you-hearing-scary-car-problem-sounds-" } </script><style> .blog-content.blog-content { font-family: 'aileron', Arial, sans-serif; color: #4b5563; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.7; display: flex; flex-direction: column; } .blog-content.blog-content >