> If your car is using more gas than it used to, the answer is not always a new car or a different commute. Sometimes the fix starts with tire pressure, the right oil, clean filters, and diagnosis of warning lights or rough running. Updated June 2026 Reading time: 8 min 3 Ways to Improve Gas Mileage With Simple Car Maintenance This guide updates our original gas-mileage post for modern vehicles. The goal is simple: show which maintenance items can help fuel economy, which claims are outdated, and when poor MPG is a symptom of a problem that needs testing. <strong>The short answer:</strong> Keep tires inflated to the door-jamb pressure, use the oil grade your manufacturer calls for, replace filters when they are dirty, and fix check-engine or rough-running problems early ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/3-ways-to-improve-gas-mileage-with-simple-car-maintenance" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<div class="blog-content"><p class="blog-index-lede">If your car is using more gas than it used to, the answer is not always a new car or a different commute. Sometimes the fix starts with tire pressure, the right oil, clean filters, and diagnosis of warning lights or rough running.</p><span class="update-badge">Updated June 2026</span><div class="post-meta"><span>Reading time: 8 min</span></div><h1>3 Ways to Improve Gas Mileage With Simple Car Maintenance</h1><p>This guide updates our original gas-mileage post for modern vehicles. The goal is simple: show which maintenance items can help fuel economy, which claims are outdated, and when poor MPG is a symptom of a problem that needs testing.</p><div class="key-takeaway"><p><strong>The short answer:</strong> Keep tires inflated to the door-jamb pressure, use the oil grade your manufacturer calls for, replace filters when they are dirty, and fix check-engine or rough-running problems early.</p><p>Maintenance will not turn a truck into a hybrid, but it can help your vehicle get closer to the fuel economy it was built to deliver.</p></div><div class="toc"><h3>In This Guide</h3><ul><li><a href="#why">Why Gas Mileage Drops</a></li><li><a href="#tires">1. Check Tire Pressure</a></li><li><a href="#oil">2. Use the Right Oil and Filters</a></li><li><a href="#diagnostics">3. Fix Engine Problems Early</a></li><li><a href="#habits">Driving Habits That Support Maintenance</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Fuel Economy Questions</a></li></ul></div><div class="trust-indicators"><div class="trust-item">Serving Aurora since 1978</div><div class="trust-item">ASE-certified mechanics</div><div class="trust-item">NAPA AutoCare Center</div><div class="trust-item">24/24 nationwide warranty</div></div><h2 id="why">Why Your Gas Mileage Gets Worse</h2><p>Fuel economy drops when the engine has to work harder, the wheels do not roll cleanly, or the computer has to compensate for a problem. Some causes are simple. Others need diagnostic testing.</p><p>Common maintenance-related causes include:</p><ul><li>Low tire pressure</li><li>Wrong oil grade or overdue oil service</li><li>Dirty engine air filter</li><li>Worn spark plugs or ignition parts</li><li>Check engine light, oxygen sensor, fuel trim, or emissions-system issues</li><li>Dragging brakes, alignment issues, or tire problems</li><li>Heavy cargo, roof racks, idling, and aggressive driving</li></ul><p>The best first step is to compare your current fuel economy to what the car normally does for your driving pattern. A sudden drop matters more than a number that has always been low for the vehicle.</p><h2 id="tires">1. Check Tire Pressure</h2><p>Tire pressure is the easiest fuel-economy check most drivers can do at home. Under-inflated tires create extra rolling resistance, which makes the engine use more fuel. They can also wear faster and affect handling.</p><p><a href="https://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/maintain.jsp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FuelEconomy.gov</a> says proper tire inflation can improve gas mileage by 0.6% on average and up to 3% in some cases. It also notes that under-inflated tires can lower MPG by about 0.2% for every 1 psi drop in the average pressure of all tires.</p><h3>How to Check Tire Pressure</h3><ul><li>Check tires cold, before a long drive.</li><li>Use the pressure on the driver's door jamb or owner's manual.</li><li>Do not use the maximum pressure printed on the tire sidewall.</li><li>Check all four tires and the spare if your vehicle has one.</li><li>Recheck after big temperature swings.</li></ul><div class="colorado-tip"><strong>Aurora Driver Tip</strong><p>Cold weather lowers tire pressure. A tire that looked fine in September may be under-inflated after the first hard freeze. Check pressure monthly and before mountain drives.</p></div><p>Tune Tech does not sell tires, but we still look at tire condition during maintenance visits because tire wear can point to alignment, suspension, brake, or inflation problems.</p><h2 id="oil">2. Use the Right Oil and Replace Filters When Needed</h2><p>Oil choice matters. Your engine is designed around a specific oil grade. Using the wrong grade can increase friction and reduce fuel economy. FuelEconomy.gov says using the manufacturer's recommended grade of motor oil can improve gas mileage by 1% to 2% compared with the wrong grade.</p><p>Oil changes also create a good checkpoint for the rest of the car. During routine service, ask for a look at belts, hoses, fluid levels, leaks, tires, lights, and visible brake condition. That quick look often catches the little things that become bigger problems later.</p><h3>What About Air Filters?</h3><p>This is where older advice needs an update. On modern fuel-injected, computer-controlled vehicles, replacing a clogged engine air filter usually improves performance more than MPG. On older carbureted vehicles, a clogged air filter can affect both performance and fuel economy.</p><p>That does not mean filters are unimportant. A dirty engine air filter can still hurt acceleration and make the engine work harder. A dirty cabin filter can weaken heat, AC, and defrost airflow. In dusty Colorado conditions, filters can load up faster than the calendar suggests.</p><p>For oil interval and oil-type details, see our <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/the-importance-of-scheduled-maintenance-why-you-shouldn-t-skip-your-next-oil-change">oil change cost guide</a>.</p><h2 id="diagnostics">3. Fix Engine Problems Early</h2><p>A vehicle with a check engine light, rough idle, hesitation, hard start, or sudden MPG drop needs diagnosis. Modern vehicles can sometimes seem to drive normally while the engine computer is compensating for a sensor, ignition, fuel, or emissions problem.</p><p>FuelEconomy.gov warns that a check engine light can point to a problem that reduces fuel economy, increases emissions, or leads to costlier repairs later. That is why the light should not be ignored just because the car still starts.</p><h3>Poor MPG Symptoms That Deserve a Look</h3><ul><li>Check engine light or flashing check engine light</li><li>Rough idle, hesitation, or stalling</li><li>Fuel smell, exhaust smell, or visible smoke</li><li>Slow starts or weak acceleration</li><li>MPG drops suddenly with no change in driving</li><li>New vibration, dragging feeling, or pull while driving</li></ul><p>A code reader gives a starting point, not the whole answer. An oxygen-sensor code, for example, can come from the sensor, wiring, exhaust leak, fuel mixture, or another engine issue. Good <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/auto-diagnostic">auto diagnostics</a> connect the code to test results before parts are replaced.</p><h2 id="habits">Driving Habits That Support Good Fuel Economy</h2><p>Maintenance matters, but driving habits still affect MPG. Smooth acceleration, steady speeds, less idling, and removing heavy cargo all help the maintenance work pay off.</p><table class="cost-table"><thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Why it helps</th><th>Simple move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Smoother starts and stops</strong></td><td>Reduces fuel use and wear on brakes and tires.</td><td>Look ahead in traffic and avoid hard launches.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Less idling</strong></td><td>Idling burns fuel while moving zero miles.</td><td>Shut the engine off during long waits when safe.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Less extra weight</strong></td><td>More weight means more work for the engine.</td><td>Remove tools, boxes, and cargo you do not need.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clean warning lights</strong></td><td>Unresolved faults can hurt MPG and emissions.</td><td>Schedule diagnostics when a light stays on.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If your car is due by mileage, our <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/scheduled-car-maintenance">factory scheduled maintenance</a> page covers 30K, 60K, 90K, 100K, and higher-mileage service planning. If it is running poorly now, our <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/tune-ups">tune-up service</a> page explains how modern tune-ups are more about targeted testing than replacing parts by habit.</p><div class="faq-section" id="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"><h2>Fuel Economy Questions, Answered</h2><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Can maintenance really improve gas mileage?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Yes, when poor MPG is connected to maintenance. Tire pressure, correct oil grade, clean filters, ignition problems, engine lights, dragging brakes, and other issues can all affect fuel use. The amount depends on the vehicle and the problem.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Does replacing an air filter improve MPG?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">On most modern fuel-injected vehicles, replacing a clogged engine air filter usually improves performance more than MPG. On older carbureted vehicles, it can improve fuel economy by a few percent. It is still worth inspecting because a dirty filter can affect drivability.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Why did my gas mileage suddenly get worse?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">A sudden MPG drop can come from low tire pressure, cold weather, heavy idling, a check engine light, ignition issues, fuel-system problems, dragging brakes, alignment issues, or a change in driving pattern. If it repeats across several fill-ups, schedule diagnostics.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">What tire pressure should I use for better MPG?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Use the tire pressure listed on the driver's door jamb or in the owner's manual. Do not inflate to the maximum number printed on the tire sidewall unless the manufacturer specifically calls for it.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Should I get a tune-up for bad gas mileage?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">A tune-up may help if the vehicle has worn spark plugs, ignition problems, dirty filters, or a maintenance item due by mileage. For modern vehicles, start with symptoms and diagnostic test results instead of replacing parts without a reason.</p></div></div></div><div class="review-section"><h2>What Aurora Drivers Say About Maintenance and MPG Problems</h2><p class="review-subtitle">Real reviews from Tune Tech customers</p><div class="review-grid"><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"After struggling for months and other repair places to determine why my mileage went from 26-27 mpg to 21-22 mpg, rough idling and intermittent engine noise, I was at the end of my rope. I decided to try one more repair shop. I'm really glad I did!"</div><div class="review-author">D. H.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"As usual Ken and his guys serviced my car quickly. Just needed an oil change, tire rotation, and general check up. Love these guys!"</div><div class="review-author">C. C.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><div class="review-text">"The Tune Tech Team once again handled my 144,000 mile old car really well. They always review any potential problems and take care of them before I find myself broke down by the side of the road."</div><div class="review-author">D. R.</div></a></div><p class="review-link"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/tmoHot2rVJYyiGTk7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View more reviews on Google &rarr;</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews">See reviews on our site &rarr;</a></p></div><div class="cta-section"><h2>Need Help Finding the Cause of Poor Gas Mileage?</h2><p>ASE-certified mechanics. NAPA AutoCare Center. Serving Aurora since 1978.</p><div class="cta-buttons"><a class="cta-primary" href="tel:3033279176">Call (303) 327-9176</a></div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json"> { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article", "headline": "3 Ways to Improve Gas Mileage With Simple Car Maintenance", "description": "Learn ho