g/improve-gas-mileage-maintenance-square.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/improve-gas-mileage-maintenance-square.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Improve gas mileage maintenance square.jpg</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[The 5 Best Holiday Gifts for Car Lovers]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/the-5-best-holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers</link><pubDate>12/9/2014 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p>&nbsp; <a href="https://www.hugedomains.com/domain_profile.cfm?d=tunetechautomotive&amp;e=com"></a> [Updated 9/26/2020] The holiday season is just around the corner, and chances are, you have a car lover or two on your shopping list. From items to use in the car and around the house to truly unique gifts that will bring a smile to a&nbsp;gearhead&rsquo;s&nbsp;face, there is plenty to choose from. Check out these unique gift ideas that are sure to be loved by anyone with a passion for automobiles. <strong>The Top 5 Holiday Gifts for Car Lovers</strong> While you&rsquo;d probably love to present the car lover in your life with his or her dream car, chances are, your&nbsp;budget won&rsquo;t allow it. Here are the top 5 holiday gifts* for car lovers that are more likely to fit into your b ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/the-5-best-holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<p>&nbsp;</p><figure><a href="https://www.hugedomains.com/domain_profile.cfm?d=tunetechautomotive&amp;e=com" title="The best holiday gifts for car lovers"><img alt="The best holiday gifts for car lovers" src="/Files/Images/Blog/holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers.jpg" /></a></figure><p>[Updated 9/26/2020] The holiday season is just around the corner, and chances are, you have a car lover or two on your shopping list. From items to use in the car and around the house to truly unique gifts that will bring a smile to a&nbsp;gearhead&rsquo;s&nbsp;face, there is plenty to choose from. Check out these unique gift ideas that are sure to be loved by anyone with a passion for automobiles.</p><h3><strong><span style="font-size:18px;">The Top 5 Holiday Gifts for Car Lovers</span></strong></h3><p>While you&rsquo;d probably love to present the car lover in your life with his or her dream car, chances are, your&nbsp;budget won&rsquo;t allow it. Here are the top 5 holiday gifts* for car lovers that are more likely to fit into your budget this holiday season.</p><h3><strong>Racing Brake Clock</strong></h3><p>For a unique gift that your car-loving recipient will look at daily, consider something like the Racing Brake Clock from AUTOart. Manufactured to the exact same size as a real racing brake, this clock is a unique conversation starter that will look great in a garage or man cave.</p><h3><strong>Smartphone Scan Tool</strong></h3><p>If you have a <b>do-it-yourselfer</b> on your shopping list, a smartphone scan tool, such as the Actron U-Scan Code Scanner is a great choice. These small devices bring all the functionality of a full-size code scanner to the user&rsquo;s smartphone. With one of these tools, <b>advanced diagnostics can be accessed by simply plugging the tool into the car&rsquo;s OBD II data port and downloading an Android or Apple app</b>. The tool syncs to the user&rsquo;s phone using BlueTooth and allows the user to read and erase codes.</p><h3><strong>Car Wash Kit</strong></h3><p>If you&rsquo;re a do-it-yourselfer yourself, consider a <b>DIY car wash kit</b>. Sure, you can buy car wash kits just about everywhere during the holiday season, but why not put together something a bit more personal? Start out with a bucket, and fill it with <b>car wash essentials</b>, like soap, glass cleaner, a wash mitt, and shop towels. Then, add in some unique goodies, like a car magazine, a kit for cleaning foggy headlights, an air freshener in his or her favorite scent, or even a mini vacuum. Use your imagination to come up with the perfect kit for the car lover in your life.</p><h3><strong>Cup Holder Tray</strong></h3><p>Know someone who spends way too much time on the road and ends up frequently eating in their car? A cup holder swivel tray is a fun gift idea. It fits in the car&rsquo;s cup holder and swivels into a comfortable position. The textured tray keeps things from sliding and spilling. When your gift recipient isn&rsquo;t using it to hold lunch, these trays are also good for keeping things like cell phones and earpieces close at hand.</p><h3><strong>Automotive Services</strong></h3><p>When you&rsquo;re looking for the ultimate practical gift for a car lover, remember that sometimes the best gifts are those that are the simplest. Help the car enthusiast in your life keep his or her ride in top condition by paying for <b>routine services like an oil change or a tune-up</b>. Call your local garage to see if they offer gift certificates or if they will allow you to pre-pay for a service for someone else. Most will be glad to help you out with giving the gift of auto service.</p><p>With a little creativity, finding the perfect holiday gifts for car lovers is easy. To give yourself the gift of a safe, reliable car, give <a href="https://plus.google.com/+TuneTechAutomotiveAurora/posts" title="Tune Tech Automotive">Tune Tech Automotive</a> a call to schedule maintenance or repairs. You can also schedule online via our website.</p><p><b>*The products listed in this guide are not manufactured or distributed by Tune Tech Automotive, nor should these suggestions be considered official endorsements. This list is for informational purposes only.</b></p> ]]></content><media:thumbnail url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Holiday gifts for car lovers.jpg</media:title></media:content><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/holiday-gifts-for-car-lovers.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">The best holiday gifts for car lovers</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[Top 5 Holiday Travel Tips]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/top-5-holiday-travel-tips</link><pubDate>12/19/2014 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p>&nbsp; <a href="/services">&nbsp;</a> [Updated 9/26/2020] If your holiday plans include a trip over the river and through the woods to spend time with family and loved ones, the last thing you need is an unpleasant surprise while you&rsquo;re on the road.&nbsp;<b>Whether you&rsquo;re heading across the country or across town, keep these holiday travel tips in mind to ensure a safe, smooth trip.</b><strong>Holiday Travel Tips</strong> The holiday season brings about some of the <a href="http://traveltips.usatoday.com/busiest-travel-day-108182.html" target="_blank">busiest travel days of the year</a>. With so many people on the road and a higher chance of inclement&nbsp;weather, there is an increased risk of unexpected problems. Fortunately, there are a lot of simple things you can do to ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/top-5-holiday-travel-tips" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<p>&nbsp;</p><figure class="align-right"><p><a href="/services" title="Holiday travel tips"><img alt="Holiday travel tips" height="280" src="/Files/Images/Blog/holiday-travel-tips.jpg" style="height: 280px; width: 500px;" width="500" />&nbsp;</a></p></figure><p>[Updated 9/26/2020] If your holiday plans include a trip over the river and through the woods to spend time with family and loved ones, the last thing you need is an unpleasant surprise while you&rsquo;re on the road.&nbsp;<b>Whether you&rsquo;re heading across the country or across town, keep these holiday travel tips in mind to ensure a safe, smooth trip.</b></p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Holiday Travel Tips</strong></span></h3><p>The holiday season brings about some of the <a href="http://traveltips.usatoday.com/busiest-travel-day-108182.html" target="_blank" title="busiest travel days of the year">busiest travel days of the year</a>. With so many people on the road and a higher chance of inclement&nbsp;weather, there is an increased risk of unexpected problems. Fortunately, there are a lot of simple things you can do to keep your family safe and avoid problems.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Do a Pre-Trip Inspection</strong></span></h3><p>Before you&rsquo;re ready to hit the road, give your car a good once over. <b>Check all your fluids</b>, including oil, windshield washer fluid, coolant, and transmission fluid. Make sure your tires are inflated to the proper pressure. <b>If you&rsquo;ve noticed any problems or your</b> check engine light is on, <b>have your car looked over by your mechanic</b>, just to be on the safe side.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Prepare for Emergencies</strong></span></h3><p>Even if you are certain your car is in perfect condition, you should always be prepared for emergencies. Check your spare tire, and make sure it&rsquo;s in good, usable condition in case you get a flat along the road. Of course, you also need to make sure you have the necessary tools to change the tire. Having a <b>basic tool kit</b> in the trunk can go a long way toward helping you make minor repairs on the road.</p><p>If you&rsquo;ll be traveling in areas impacted by harsh winter weather, you should take a <b>basic winter survival kit</b>, too. Blankets, a flashlight, bottled water, and non-perishable food items should always be in the car, just in case. Some extra cash, or money reserved on an emergency credit card, will also come in handy if you find yourself in need of repairs or a hotel room for the night.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Don&rsquo;t Travel During Peak Travel Times</strong></span></h3><p>If possible, <b>schedule your trip on less busy days</b>. If you must travel on the major travel days, opt to <b>leave early in the morning or late at night</b> so you can beat the heaviest traffic. Not only will you save time, but you will also be less likely to be involved in an accident. If you&rsquo;re going on a long trip that requires several hours,&nbsp;or even days,&nbsp;on the road, plan ahead so that you will pass through major cities during less busy hours to avoid getting caught up in rush hour traffic.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Plan Ahead</strong></span></h3><p>Don&rsquo;t rely on just putting your destination in your GPS the morning of your departure. <b>Plan your route out ahead of time</b>. Doing so will let you know how long it will take you to get from Point A to Point B, and it will also give you an opportunity to plan your stops along the way. If you&rsquo;re traveling to or through rural areas, this could be especially helpful in allowing you to <b>determine where to stop to rest for the night or to fill up the tank</b> when hotels and gas stations are few and far between.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Take Frequent Breaks</strong></span></h3><p><b>Driving during the winter months can be much more exhausting than summer driving</b>. Don&rsquo;t let your desire to get to your destination in a hurry keep you from taking the breaks your body needs. Just stopping for a few minutes to stand up and stretch your legs goes a long way towards&nbsp;keeping you awake and alert.</p><p>Whether your holiday travels are taking you around the block or around the country, I would like to extend warm wishes for a safe and happy holiday from all of us here at <a href="https://plus.google.com/+TuneTechAutomotiveAurora/&gt;Tune " target="_blank" title="Tech Automotive">Tech Automotive</a>. If you need any help getting your car road trip ready or you need some repairs when you get home, we&rsquo;re here to help. Give us a call.</p> ]]></content><media:thumbnail url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/holiday-travel-tips.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/holiday-travel-tips.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Holiday travel tips.jpg</media:title></media:content><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/holiday-travel-tips.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">Holiday travel tips</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[3 New Year’s Resolutions For Your Car]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/3-new-year-s-resolutions-for-your-car</link><pubDate>1/12/2015 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p>[Updated 9/26/2020] It&rsquo;s that time of year again; The new year is officially upon us, and you&rsquo;ve likely spent at least a little bit of time during the past couple weeks thinking about ways to improve yourself in the new year. But why limit <b>New Year&rsquo;s Resolutions</b> to just yourself? <b>Right now is a great time to set some goals for maintaining your vehicle</b>, too! &nbsp; Start the year off on the right foot with these New Year&rsquo;s resolutions for your car! <strong>New Year&rsquo;s Resolutions For Your Car</strong> It may seem silly to think about making resolutions for your car, but look at it this way: When you make resolutions for yourself, they probably include things like improving your diet, losing weight, or better managing finances, all of which are g ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/3-new-year-s-resolutions-for-your-car" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<p>[Updated 9/26/2020]</p><p>It&rsquo;s that time of year again; The new year is officially upon us, and you&rsquo;ve likely spent at least a little bit of time during the past couple weeks thinking about ways to improve yourself in the new year. But why limit <b>New Year&rsquo;s Resolutions</b> to just yourself? <b>Right now is a great time to set some goals for maintaining your vehicle</b>, too!</p><div style="page-break-after: always"><span style="display: none;">&nbsp;</span></div><!--nextpage--><figure class="align-right"><img alt="3 New Year’s Resolutions For Your Car" height="299" src="/Files/Images/Blog/new-years-resolutions-for-your-car.jpg" style="height: 299px; width: 450px; border-width: 0px; border-style: solid;" width="450" /><figcaption>Start the year off on the right foot with these New Year&rsquo;s resolutions for your car!</figcaption></figure><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>New Year&rsquo;s Resolutions For Your Car</strong></span></h3><p>It may seem silly to think about making resolutions for your car, but look at it this way: When you make resolutions for yourself, they probably include things like improving your diet, losing weight, or better managing finances, all of which are goals that help ensure your future well-being. You can also set goals to ensure the long life of your car, and the new year is a great time to vow to break bad <strong>vehicle maintenance</strong> habits, like going too long between <strong>oil changes</strong>.</p><p>Check out the top <strong>3 New Year&rsquo;s Resolutions for your car</strong> recommended by all of us here at Tune Tech Automotive.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Clean Up Your Act</strong></span></h3><p>You may have added cleaning up and organizing your home to your list of New Year&rsquo;s resolutions, but what about cleaning up your car? Remove all the excess junk and clutter from inside your car. <a href="http://www.fueleconomy.gov/feg/driveHabits.jsp" target="_blank" title="Getting rid of excess weight improves gas mileage">Getting rid of excess weight improves gas mileage</a>. <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/3-ways-to-improve-gas-mileage-with-simple-car-maintenance">Read more gas mileage tips here.</a></p><p>If you&rsquo;re a habitual clutterbug, make it a personal goal to keep trash and junk out of your car this year. Keeping your car tidy can even help preserve retail value as the carpet and upholstery are less likely to become stained. Keep the outside of your vehicle clean, too. In addition to looking better, keeping your car&rsquo;s exterior clean helps <strong>prevent rust</strong>.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Learn to Be More Proactive</strong></span></h3><p>The best way to keep your car running its best and avoid costly problems is to <strong>stay on top of routine maintenance</strong>. From routine oil changes and checking your tire pressure to keeping up with the <b>scheduled maintenance</b> recommended by your vehicle&rsquo;s manufacturer, these steps can help you avoid breakdowns.</p><p>If you do notice something unusual, such as a strange sound or a change in the way your car drives, vow not to put off going to the garage. No one wants to be saddled with a huge repair bill, but if you bring your car to Tune Tech Automotive at the first sign of trouble, the expense will likely be much less than if you wait until the issue becomes worse.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Be a Better Driver</strong></span></h3><p><b>The way you drive has a huge impact on how long your car will last</b>. Your driving habits also can help or hinder your gas mileage. <b>Sudden accelerations burn excessive gas</b>, as does <b>speeding</b>. Aggressive stopping and <b>slamming on your brakes causes the braking material to wear down</b> faster than it does when braking gradually. Of course, driving aggressively or recklessly also puts you at a much <b>higher risk of having an accident</b>. So, in 2015, make it a resolution to <b>be a safer, more careful driver</b>. Obey the speed limit, and avoid aggressive acceleration and braking as much as possible.</p><h3><span style="font-size:18px;"><strong>Stick to Your Resolutions with Tune Tech Automotive</strong></span></h3><p>At Tune Tech Automotive, we can help you stick with the New Year&rsquo;s resolutions you set for your car this year. We can handle your routine maintenance, and we will be here&nbsp;any time you need us for repairs. Let us help you take the best possible care of your vehicle. <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/appointments">Click here to schedule your appointment&nbsp;now.</a></p> ]]></content><media:thumbnail url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/new-years-resolutions-for-your-car.jpg" /><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/EmailCampaigns/new-years-resolutions-for-your-car.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">New years resolutions for your car.jpg</media:title></media:content><media:content url="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/Files/Images/Blog/new-years-resolutions-for-your-car.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="html">3 New Year’s Resolutions For Your Car</media:title></media:content></item><item><title><![CDATA[5 Tips For Getting Your Car Ready For Summer]]></title><link>https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/5-tips-for-getting-your-car-ready-for-summer</link><pubDate>5/15/2015 12:00:00 AM</pubDate><category><![CDATA[Car Problems & Diagnostics]]></category><category><![CDATA[Maintenance & Care]]></category><category><![CDATA[Shop Announcements]]></category><category><![CDATA[Gift Ideas]]></category><category><![CDATA[Repair Cost Guides]]></category><description><![CDATA[<p> Colorado summer driving means hot Colfax traffic, afternoon storms, I-70 climbs, mountain descents, road trips, and back-to-school routines. That mix is hard on A/C, cooling, tires, brakes, fluids, belts, hoses, and batteries. Updated June 2026 Reading time: 8 min 5 Ways to Get Your Car Ready for Summer in Colorado That mix is hard on the same parts that often get ignored until they fail: A/C, cooling system, tires, brakes, fluids, belts, hoses, wipers, battery, and filters. <strong>The short answer:</strong> Before summer travel or a school-year routine, check the systems that protect you from heat, stopping trouble, visibility problems, and roadside breakdowns. If the A/C is warm, the temperature gauge is moving, the brake pedal feels different, or your tires are l ... <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/5-tips-for-getting-your-car-ready-for-summer" title="Read more ..." class="link link-read-more">read more</a></p>]]></description><content><![CDATA[<div class="blog-content"><p class="blog-index-lede">Colorado summer driving means hot Colfax traffic, afternoon storms, I-70 climbs, mountain descents, road trips, and back-to-school routines. That mix is hard on A/C, cooling, tires, brakes, fluids, belts, hoses, and batteries.</p><span class="update-badge">Updated June 2026</span><div class="post-meta"><span>Reading time: 8 min</span></div><h1>5 Ways to Get Your Car Ready for Summer in Colorado</h1><p>That mix is hard on the same parts that often get ignored until they fail: A/C, cooling system, tires, brakes, fluids, belts, hoses, wipers, battery, and filters.</p><div class="key-takeaway"><p><strong>The short answer:</strong> Before summer travel or a school-year routine, check the systems that protect you from heat, stopping trouble, visibility problems, and roadside breakdowns.</p><p>If the A/C is warm, the temperature gauge is moving, the brake pedal feels different, or your tires are low or worn, do not wait for a long drive to test it.</p></div><div class="toc"><h3>In This Guide</h3><ul><li><a href="#ac">1. Test the A/C and Cabin Airflow</a></li><li><a href="#cooling">2. Check Cooling System, Belts, and Hoses</a></li><li><a href="#tires">3. Inspect Tires, Wipers, and Visibility</a></li><li><a href="#brakes">4. Check Brakes Before Mountain Driving</a></li><li><a href="#fluids">5. Handle Fluids, Battery, Filters, and School-Year Basics</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Summer Car Care Questions</a></li></ul></div><div class="trust-indicators"><div class="trust-item">Serving Aurora since 1978</div><div class="trust-item">ASE-certified mechanics</div><div class="trust-item">NAPA AutoCare Center</div><div class="trust-item">24/24 nationwide warranty</div></div><h2 id="ac">1. Test the A/C and Cabin Airflow</h2><p>Turn the A/C on before the first heat wave. Cold air should start coming through the vents within a short drive. If the air is warm, weak, musty, or fades after a few minutes, the system needs more than a quick guess.</p><p>Warm A/C can come from a refrigerant leak, compressor issue, electrical problem, fan problem, blend-door issue, clogged cabin air filter, or a control problem. Recharging the system over and over without finding the leak can waste money and may leave you with the same hot-air problem in July.</p><p>A/C refrigerant service is regulated work. The <a href="https://www.epa.gov/mvac" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EPA's motor vehicle air-conditioning service page</a> notes that paid MVAC service requires proper Section 609 training and certified equipment. That is one reason a real A/C diagnosis matters when the system is low or leaking.</p><div class="colorado-tip"><strong>Quick check:</strong><p>Run the A/C for a few minutes every few weeks, even outside summer. It helps circulate oil through the system. If you have not used it since last fall, test it before you need it.</p></div><p>If the A/C is not keeping up, start with our <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/ac-heating-repair">A/C and heating repair</a> page or the <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/blog/how-much-does-car-ac-repair-cost-in-aurora">Aurora A/C repair cost guide</a>.</p><h2 id="cooling">2. Check Cooling System, Belts, and Hoses</h2><p>Your engine cooling system has to move heat away from the engine all year, but summer traffic and mountain driving raise the load. The most important rule is simple: never open a hot cooling system. Check coolant only when the engine is cold.</p><p>Look for coolant level, coolant color, leaks under the vehicle, sweet smells, steam, and a temperature gauge that climbs higher than normal. Hoses should feel firm but flexible. A hose that is cracked, swollen, mushy, or wet near the clamp can fail when heat and pressure rise.</p><p>Belts matter too. A worn serpentine belt can affect the alternator, power steering, water pump on some vehicles, and A/C operation. Look for cracks, fraying, glazing, squealing, or a battery warning light.</p><div class="warning-box"><p><strong>Stop-driving signs:</strong> pull over safely if the temperature warning comes on, the gauge climbs into the red, steam appears, or coolant is leaking heavily. Continuing to drive an overheating engine can turn a smaller cooling-system repair into major engine damage.</p></div><p>A summer check can be paired with <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/scheduled-car-maintenance">scheduled car maintenance</a> so coolant, belts, hoses, oil, and inspection items are handled in one visit.</p><h2 id="tires">3. Inspect Tires, Wipers, and Visibility</h2><p>Summer road trips are tire trips. Hot pavement, highway speeds, potholes, construction zones, and mountain grades all make tire condition matter.</p><p>The <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle-safety/tires" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHTSA TireWise</a> guidance says poor tire maintenance can lead to flats, blowouts, or tread separation. It recommends checking all tires, including the spare, at least once a month when the tires are cold.</p><p>Use the tire pressure on the driver-side door label or owner manual, not the number molded into the tire sidewall. Check tread depth, uneven wear, sidewall cracks, bulges, vibration, and any tire-pressure warning light. Tune Tech does not sell tires, but tire condition can still point to alignment, suspension, brake, or maintenance problems that need attention.</p><p>Wipers belong in this same check. Colorado sun can dry and split blades, and summer storms can go from dry pavement to low visibility fast. Replace streaking or chattering wipers before rain, hail, or early fall weather catches you.</p><h2 id="brakes">4. Check Brakes Before Mountain Driving</h2><p>Brakes that feel fine in town can feel very different after a long downhill grade. If your summer includes I-70, foothill drives, camping, or a loaded vehicle, have brake symptoms checked before the trip.</p><p>Watch for squealing, grinding, vibration, pulling to one side, a soft brake pedal, a pedal that travels farther than usual, or a burning smell after braking. Brake fluid also matters. Old or contaminated fluid can make braking less consistent under heat.</p><p>If you are already hearing brake noise, use the trip as the deadline, not the test. Our <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/brakes">brake repair</a> page explains the service path, and the brake warning-sign guide covers symptoms in more detail.</p><h2 id="fluids">5. Handle Fluids, Battery, Filters, and School-Year Basics</h2><p>The old back-to-school checklist had the right instinct: a car that handles daily drop-off, commuting, college drives, and after-school activities needs more than A/C. Late summer is a good time to catch ordinary maintenance before the schedule gets crowded.</p><table class="cost-table"><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Why It Matters</th><th>Warning Sign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine oil</td><td>Protects the engine in heat and stop-and-go driving</td><td>Oil light, overdue service, dark or low oil</td></tr><tr><td>Brake fluid</td><td>Supports firm, consistent braking</td><td>Soft pedal, dark fluid, leak warning</td></tr><tr><td>Washer fluid and wipers</td><td>Keeps visibility clear during storms and road grime</td><td>Streaks, chatter, low fluid, cracked blades</td></tr><tr><td>Battery and charging</td><td>Heat can expose a weak battery before cold weather does</td><td>Slow crank, battery light, corrosion, repeated jump starts</td></tr><tr><td>Engine and cabin filters</td><td>Help airflow, comfort, and normal engine operation</td><td>Weak vent airflow, odor, dirty filter, rough running</td></tr><tr><td>Suspension and steering</td><td>Keeps the vehicle stable over rough roads and potholes</td><td>Pulling, clunking, vibration, uneven tire wear</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you are already due for an <a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/oil-change">oil change</a>, ask for the summer and road-trip items at the same time. It is easier to handle small maintenance items in one visit than to chase them one at a time after a trip starts.</p><div class="review-section"><h2>What Aurora Drivers Say</h2><p class="review-subtitle">Real review excerpts from Tune Tech Automotive customers.</p><div class="review-grid"><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><p class="review-text">"I was at a light and my engine light came on saying that it was overheating. I was able to get it cooled down enough to get it to the facility."</p><div class="review-author">K. C.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><p class="review-text">"He took care of my brakes and my AC now blows really cold air."</p><div class="review-author">A. D.</div></a><a class="review-card" href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews"><div class="review-stars">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div><p class="review-text">"Ken and his staff got my van ready for a summer trip... my van will operate safely and correctly during a road trip."</p><div class="review-author">D. K.</div></a></div><p class="review-link"><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/tmoHot2rVJYyiGTk7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View more reviews on Google &rarr;</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp;<a href="https://www.tunetechautomotive.org/reviews">See reviews on our site &rarr;</a></p></div><div class="faq-section" id="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage"><h2>Summer Car Care Questions</h2><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">What should I check before a summer road trip?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Check A/C performance, coolant level, visible leaks, tire pressure, tire tread, brakes, oil, washer fluid, wipers, belts, hoses, battery condition, and warning lights. If the trip includes mountain driving, pay extra attention to brakes, cooling, and tire condition.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Why is my car A/C blowing warm air?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Warm A/C can come from low refrigerant, a leak, compressor trouble, electrical faults, fan problems, blend-door issues, or a clogged cabin filter. The right fix starts with diagnosis, especially if the system has already been recharged before.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">How often should I check tire pressure in summer?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Check tire pressure at least once a month when the tires are cold, and check before long trips. Use the pressure listed on the driver-side door label or owner manual. Do not use the maximum pressure printed on the tire sidewall as the normal setting.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Should I do summer maintenance before school starts?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">Yes, late summer is a good time to combine oil service, tire pressure, wipers, brakes, fluids, filters, battery, and heating or A/C checks. That helps the car handle school drop-off, commuting, college drives, and the first cold mornings later in the season.</p></div></div><div class="faq-item" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question"><h3 itemprop="name">Can I check these items myself?</h3><div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer"><p itemprop="text">You can check tire pressure, wipers, lights, warning lights, visible leaks, and some fluid levels at home. Have a shop check symptoms such as warm A/C, overheating, brake noise, soft brake pedal, battery trouble, steering noise, or leaks you cannot identify.</p></div></div></div><div class="cta-section"><h2>Get Your Car Ready Before the Heat or the Trip</h2><p>Call Tune Tech Automotive in Aurora for summer maintenance, A/C diagnosis, cooling-system checks, brakes, oil service, and road-trip inspections.</p><div class="cta-buttons"><a class="cta-primary" href="tel:3033279176">Call (303) 327-9176</a></div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json"> { "@context": "https://sch